import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Interrupts and exceptions: IDT, TSS, IST, LAPIC","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"arch/interrupts.md","filePath":"arch/interrupts.md"}'),c={name:"arch/interrupts.md"};function s(d,e,i,n,l,h){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r(`<h1 id="interrupts-and-exceptions-idt-tss-ist-lapic" tabindex="-1">Interrupts and exceptions: IDT, TSS, IST, LAPIC <a class="header-anchor" href="#interrupts-and-exceptions-idt-tss-ist-lapic" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Interrupts and exceptions: IDT, TSS, IST, LAPIC&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Everything interrupt-shaped in one place: the descriptor tables, the vector map, the exception dispatch path with its IST discipline, and the LAPIC/MSI layer. Files: <code>ocos/kernel/arch/x86_64/arch_idt.{c,h}</code>, <code>arch_idt_stub.S</code>, <code>arch_idt_load.S</code>, <code>arch_exceptions.{c,h}</code>, <code>arch_irq.{c,h}</code>, <code>arch_pic.h</code>, <code>arch_lapic.{c,h}</code>, <code>arch_exc_exit.S</code>, <code>arch_abi_probe.S</code>.</p><h2 id="gdt-tss" tabindex="-1">GDT / TSS <a class="header-anchor" href="#gdt-tss" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;GDT / TSS&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A 64-byte GDT (<code>arch_gdt_init</code> in <code>arch_idt.c</code>):</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span> 0 = null          (sel 0x00)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span> 1 = code32        (sel 0x08)  P|ring0|code|RX, 4K|32-bit</span></span>
<span class="line"><span> 2 = data          (sel 0x10)  P|ring0|data|RW</span></span>
<span class="line"><span> 3 = code64        (sel 0x18)  P|ring0|code|RX, 4K|64-bit (L=1)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span> 4 = TSS (16-byte) (sel 0x20)  P|ring0|system|TSS64-available (0x89)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span> 6 = user code64   (sel 0x30, pushed as 0x33 with RPL 3)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span> 7 = user data     (sel 0x38, pushed as 0x3B with RPL 3)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The TSS (<code>arch_tss_t</code>, packed) carries:</p><ul><li><strong>rsp0</strong> - updated on every task switch (the ring-3 interrupt entry stack; each task&#39;s kernel stack top lives in <code>task_t.rsp0</code>).</li><li><strong>IST1 = 8 KiB <code>g_panic_stack</code></strong> - used by vectors 8 (#DF) and 18 (#MC).</li><li><strong>IST2 = 8 KiB <code>g_debug_stack</code></strong> - used by vectors 1 (#DB), 2 (NMI), 3 (#BP).</li><li><strong>iopb_offset at 0x66</strong> per SDM table 8-2 with a 32-byte all-0xFF IOPB inside the TSS limit -&gt; <strong>every port [0, 256) is denied from ring 3</strong> (the PRIV_TEST root-cause fix: <code>in</code>/<code>out</code> from user mode now faults instead of silently working).</li></ul><p><code>arch_load_gdt_idt_tr</code> (<code>arch_idt_load.S</code>): lgdt + lidt + segment reloads + a far <code>retfq</code> CS reload + <code>ltr 0x20</code>.</p><h2 id="idt" tabindex="-1">IDT <a class="header-anchor" href="#idt" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;IDT&quot;">​</a></h2><p>256 gates from the <code>arch_isr_table</code> stub array: selector 0x18, flags <strong>0x8E</strong> (P | ring0 | interrupt gate) - except vector 0x80 (syscall) and vector 3 (#BP) which get <strong>0xEE (DPL=3)</strong> so ring 3 can <code>int 0x80</code> / <code>int3</code>. IST1 for vectors 8 and 18; IST2 for vectors 1, 2, 3. <code>arch_idt_load</code> also sets EFER.NXE (MSR 0xC0000080 bit 11).</p><p>The stubs (<code>arch_idt_stub.S</code>, NASM macro x 256): vectors with CPU error codes (8, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 17, 30) skip the fake error-code push; the common path pushes 15 GPRs, executes <code>cld</code> (BUG-0138 - ABI requires DF=0 at C entry), dynamically aligns the stack to 16 bytes around <code>call arch_isr_dispatch</code> (BUG-0137), and <code>iretq</code>s.</p><h2 id="vector-map" tabindex="-1">Vector map <a class="header-anchor" href="#vector-map" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Vector map&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Vectors</th><th>Use</th><th>EOI</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0..31</td><td>CPU exceptions -&gt; <code>arch_exc_dispatch</code></td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>32..47 (0x20..0x2F)</td><td>8259 PIC IRQ0..15 -&gt; <code>arch_irq_dispatch</code></td><td>early EOI</td></tr><tr><td>48..111 (0x30..0x6F)</td><td>MSI/MSI-X -&gt; <code>arch_msi_dispatch</code></td><td>LAPIC EOI</td></tr><tr><td>128 (0x80)</td><td>syscall (DPL=3) -&gt; <code>core_syscall_dispatch</code></td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>0xFF</td><td>LAPIC spurious (counted, dropped)</td><td>-</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 8259s are remapped so IRQ0-15 land on vectors 32-47. The LAPIC is enabled xAPIC-style (MMIO at 0xFEE00000) with EOI, self-IPI and MSI/MSI-X vector dispatch 0x30..0x6F (4 handler slots per vector); <code>msitest</code> arms a real e1000 MSI and counts LAPIC vector hits (<a href="./driver-model.html">driver-model.md</a>). The <strong>early-EOI policy</strong> on both PIC and MSI paths exists so a context switch inside a handler can never wedge the interrupt controller.</p><p>Exception names (<code>arch_exc_names[32]</code>): <code>&quot;#DE Divide Error&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#DB Debug&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;NMI Non-Maskable Interrupt&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#BP Breakpoint&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#OF Overflow&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#BR Bound Range Exceeded&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#UD Invalid Opcode&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#NM Device Not Available&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#DF Double Fault&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#TS Invalid TSS&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#NP Segment Not Present&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#SS Stack-Segment Fault&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#GP General Protection&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#PF Page Fault&quot;</code>, ... <code>&quot;#MF x87 FPU&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#AC Alignment Check&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#MC Machine Check&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#XF SIMD FP&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#SX Security Exception&quot;</code>.</p><h2 id="registration-interfaces" tabindex="-1">Registration interfaces <a class="header-anchor" href="#registration-interfaces" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Registration interfaces&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Exceptions: <code>arch_exc_register_handler(vector, handler)</code> - up to 4 handlers per vector (<code>OC_EXC_CHAIN_LEN 4</code>), most-recently-registered runs FIRST; a handler returning 1 claims the fault and execution resumes.</li><li>IRQs: <code>arch_irq_register_handler(irq 0..15, fn, ctx)</code> - 4 slots per IRQ, auto-PIC-unmask; unregister compacts and re-masks when empty.</li><li>MSI: <code>arch_lapic_register_vector(0x30..0x6F, fn, ctx)</code>.</li><li>Page faults: <code>mem_vmm_register_fault_handler(fn)</code> - up to 4 (<a href="./memory.html">memory.md</a>).</li><li>Per-vector counters (<code>arch_irq_counts[256]</code> / <code>arch_exc_counts[256]</code>) back the <code>exc</code> and <code>irqabitest</code> diagnostics.</li></ul><h2 id="exception-dispatch-arch-exc-dispatch" tabindex="-1">Exception dispatch (<code>arch_exc_dispatch</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#exception-dispatch-arch-exc-dispatch" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Exception dispatch (\`arch_exc_dispatch\`)&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Nested-#PF guard</strong>: a #PF raised while already handling one prints (serial only) <code>*** NESTED #PF INSIDE EXCEPTION HANDLER - HALTING ***</code> and cli;hlt forever.</li><li>The L1 handler chain (reverse registration order).</li><li>#PF -&gt; <code>mem_vmm_handle_page_fault</code> (stack growth etc.); resume if handled.</li><li>Serial dump: <code>*** EXCEPTION &lt;v&gt;: &lt;name&gt; ***</code> with error_code/rip/ cs/rflags/rsp/ss (+ cr2 for #PF).</li><li><strong>Ring-3 origin</strong> (<code>cs &amp; 3 == 3</code>): SIGSEGV delivery attempt (handler + not-in-handler + &lt; 3 deliveries; the restorer trampoline <code>mov eax,42; int 0x80; hlt</code> is written on-stack at stack_base+0x400, BUG-0277); on failure or exhaustion: crashlog record + <code>*** KILLING user process (pid=N) due to exception V, kernel continues ***</code>, unified reaper, parent wake, TASK_EXITED, yield. <strong>For vectors 1/2/3 the kill runs on the dedicated exit stack</strong> - see IST discipline below.</li><li><strong>Ring-0 origin</strong> -&gt; panic with stack-overflow attribution: <code>#PF</code> with CR2 inside a task&#39;s guard page -&gt; <code>&quot;KERNEL STACK OVERFLOW (guard page hit, task=&lt;name&gt;)&quot;</code>; <code>#DF</code> with rsp at/below the task stack bottom or implausible garbage (&gt;= 0x40000000000) -&gt; <code>&quot;KERNEL STACK OVERFLOW (stack exhausted -&gt; #DF, task=&lt;name&gt;)&quot;</code>; otherwise <code>&quot;ring-0 exception &lt;v&gt; (&lt;name&gt;)&quot;</code>. -&gt; <code>kernel_panic_frame(reason, f)</code> (<a href="./crash-recovery.html">crash-recovery.md</a>).</li></ol><h2 id="ist-discipline-the-part-you-cannot-see" tabindex="-1">IST discipline (the part you cannot see) <a class="header-anchor" href="#ist-discipline-the-part-you-cannot-see" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;IST discipline (the part you cannot see)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>IST1/IST2 give #DF/#MC/#DB/NMI/#BP stacks that nothing else can touch</p><ul><li>but that creates a constraint: the scheduler must never save an rsp <em>inside</em> IST2. A ring-3 kill taken on vector 1/2/3 would do exactly that (reap + yield from the IST2 frame). The fix (BUG-0139, <code>arch_exc_exit.S</code>): <code>arch_exc_run_on_exit_stack()</code> switches to a dedicated 8 KiB exit stack <em>before</em> the reap+yield, so IST2 is never suspended or resumed. Same family of discipline: the <code>#DF</code> panic path runs entirely at dispatcher depth with one static 192-byte serial line buffer - no record, no dump, no flush for #DF (stated in KNOWN_ISSUES), because by definition the machine is already on its last stack.</li></ul><h2 id="the-abi-probe-arch-abi-probe-s" tabindex="-1">The ABI probe (<code>arch_abi_probe.S</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-abi-probe-arch-abi-probe-s" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The ABI probe (\`arch_abi_probe.S\`)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>irqabitest</code> arms <code>g_arch_irq_entry_probe</code> - the first action in <code>arch_isr_dispatch</code> jumps to the probe, which verifies that at C dispatch entry rsp is 8 mod 16 (the SysV alignment rule with the call-return address pushed) and DF is 0, accumulating result bits and self-healing DF. This is the regression test for the BUG-0137/0138 alignment/cld fixes above - the ABI contract between the stubs and the C world is <em>tested</em>, not assumed.</p><h2 id="timer-plumbing" tabindex="-1">Timer plumbing <a class="header-anchor" href="#timer-plumbing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Timer plumbing&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The PIT (8253/8254 ch0 @ 100 Hz, divisor 11932, mode 2, ports 0x40/0x43) raises IRQ0; <code>core_timer.c</code> bumps the tick/ms counters and runs the 16-slot soft-timer table (periodic + one-shot, callbacks in IRQ context). Tick consumers: the scheduler slice, the net stack&#39;s 10 ms poll, the health sampler&#39;s 1 s, the watchdog checks, the boot-state heartbeat (<a href="./scheduler.html">scheduler.md</a>, <a href="./network-stack.html">network-stack.md</a>). HPET interrupts (when tested) ride the <strong>legacy route timer1 -&gt; IRQ8</strong> because the kernel drives only the two PICs - timer0 would collide with the PIT on IRQ0 and is never enabled. RTC UIE/PIE/AIE also share IRQ8 through the chain table.</p><h2 id="s3-suspend-round-trip" tabindex="-1">S3 suspend round-trip <a class="header-anchor" href="#s3-suspend-round-trip" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;S3 suspend round-trip&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>arch_acpi_s3.S</code> saves RSP/CR3/CR4/CR0 into a fixed low-memory save area (offsets 0x200/0x208/0x20C/0x210) and writes PM1a_CNT SLP_TYP|SLP_EN. <code>arch_acpi_s3_resume.S</code> is the 16/32-bit resume trampoline: restores control regs, re-enters long mode, restores RSP, returns into the C caller. After firmware reset: <code>arch_irq_rearm</code> (PIC), <code>core_timer_resume</code> (PIT), <code>driver_pci_restore_commands</code> (IO+MEM+BUS_MASTER on every device) and the e1000e register replay - <a href="./driver-model.html">driver-model.md</a>.</p>`,28)])])}const m=t(c,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
